### Site specifics 

<small style="width: 95%; position: relative; bottom: 80px;">

There are minor differences between the sites. 

- CPU/GPU types
- installed modules
- interactivity

Examples: Tetralith (NSC), Dardel (KTH), Kebnekaise (HPC2N) 

-----

### Site specifics - Tetralith 

<normal>

- 32 cores/node
- 96-384 GB RAM/node
- Nvidia T4 GPUs (1/node) 
    - 
```bash 
#SBATCH -n 1
#SBATCH -c 32
#SBATCH --gpus-per-task=1
```

-----

### Site specifics - Dardel 

- 128 cores/node
- 256-2048 GB RAM/node
- 4 AMD Instinct™ MI250X á 2 GCDs per node 
    - 
```bash 
#SBATCH -N 1
#SBATCH --ntasks-per-node=1
#SBATCH -p gpu
```

-----

#### Site specifics - Kebnekaise 

<small style="width: 95%; position: relative; bottom: 80px;">

- 28 cores/skylake, 72 cores/largemem, 128/256 cores/AMD Zen3/Zen4 
- 128-3072 GB RAM/node 
- NVidia V100, A100, A6000, L40s, H100, A40, AMD MI100 
    - 
```bash 
#SBATCH --gpus=x
#SBATCH -C <type>
```
    - type and number of cards/node (x) 
        - v100, 2
        - a40, 8
        - a6000, 2
        - l40s, 2 or 6
        - a100, 2
        - h100, 4
        - mi100, 2


-----

### Serial, MPI, and OpenMP jobs

Serial job: 

```bash 
#!/bin/bash 
#SBATCH -A <account>
#SBATCH -t HHH:MM:SS 
#SBATCH -n 1

module load <modules>

./myserialprogram  
```

- n, number of tasks. Since cores per task is 1 as default, this equals cores in this case. 

-----

#### Note: Time/walltime

<small style="width: 95%; position: relative; bottom: 80px;"> 

- the job **will** terminate when the time runs out, whether it has finished or not 
- you will only be "charged" for the consumed time
- asking for more time than needed will make the job take longer to start
- short jobs can start quicker (backfill)
- if you have no idea how long your job takes, ask for "long" time 
- Conclusion: Ask for "a bit" more time than needed, but not too much

-----

### Serial, MPI, and OpenMP jobs

<small style="width: 95%; position: relative; bottom: 80px;"> 

MPI job: 

```bash
#!/bin/bash 
#SBATCH -A <account>
#SBATCH -t HHH:MM:SS 
#SBATCH -n <tasks> 

module load <modules>

srun ./mympiprogram
```

- Other options are asking for whole nodes (- N) and possibly asking for ``--tasks-per-node`` 
- ``srun`` and ``mpirun`` should be interchangeable at HPC2N. Tetralith uses ``mpprun`` and Dardel uses ``srun`` 
- Remember you need to load a module with MPI 

-----

### Serial, MPI, and OpenMP jobs

OpenMP job: 

```bash
#!/bin/bash 
#SBATCH -A <account>
#SBATCH -t HHH:MM:SS 
#SBATCH -c <cores-per-task> 

module load <modules>

# Set OMP_NUM_THREADS to the same value as -c with a fallback in case it isn't set.
# SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK is set to the value of -c, but only if -c is explicitly set
if [ -n "$SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK" ]; then
  omp_threads=$SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK
else
  omp_threads=1
fi
export OMP_NUM_THREADS=$omp_threads

./myopenmpprogram
```

